\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {R.10}{\ignorespaces Distribution of the cost-optimal next-month NPI across $228$ (region, prefix) decision points, broken down by humanitarian weight $w$. The optimum shifts monotonically from $\{1,5\}$ at $w=0$ to $\{6,7\}$ at $w=1$. NPI~$3$ (lifting mass-gathering restrictions while keeping travel and work restricted) is the only one of the seven categories that is essentially never selected: a strictly Pareto-dominated alternative on the simulated grid.}}{18}{table.caption.12}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:state_action_action_distribution}{{R.10}{18}{Distribution of the cost-optimal next-month NPI across $228$ (region, prefix) decision points, broken down by humanitarian weight $w$. The optimum shifts monotonically from $\{1,5\}$ at $w=0$ to $\{6,7\}$ at $w=1$. NPI~$3$ (lifting mass-gathering restrictions while keeping travel and work restricted) is the only one of the seven categories that is essentially never selected: a strictly Pareto-dominated alternative on the simulated grid}{table.caption.12}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {R.11}{\ignorespaces Five managerially informative leaves of the pooled state-action tree (depth~$4$, minimum leaf size~$20$, $1{,}140$ training points). None of the conditions references a region indicator or a region-specific covariate. R1--R3 jointly cover $359$ of the $1{,}140$ training points and characterise the modal high-$w$, exit, and lockdown regimes; R4 and R5 break the obvious ``high $w \Rightarrow $ stricter NPI'' and ``low $w \Rightarrow $ looser NPI'' heuristics.}}{19}{table.caption.13}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:state_action_rules}{{R.11}{19}{Five managerially informative leaves of the pooled state-action tree (depth~$4$, minimum leaf size~$20$, $1{,}140$ training points). None of the conditions references a region indicator or a region-specific covariate. R1--R3 jointly cover $359$ of the $1{,}140$ training points and characterise the modal high-$w$, exit, and lockdown regimes; R4 and R5 break the obvious ``high $w \Rightarrow $ stricter NPI'' and ``low $w \Rightarrow $ looser NPI'' heuristics}{table.caption.13}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Pooled state-action decision tree with humanitarian weight $w$ as a feature (depth~$4$, minimum leaf size~$20$, five-fold CV accuracy~$0.39$). Internal nodes split on the seven observable state features and on $w$; leaves predict the optimal next-month NPI, colour-coded by class. All $15$ leaves have at least $20$ training points.}}{19}{figure.caption.14}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:state_action_pooled_tree}{{3}{19}{Pooled state-action decision tree with humanitarian weight $w$ as a feature (depth~$4$, minimum leaf size~$20$, five-fold CV accuracy~$0.39$). Internal nodes split on the seven observable state features and on $w$; leaves predict the optimal next-month NPI, colour-coded by class. All $15$ leaves have at least $20$ training points}{figure.caption.14}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Cost-optimal next-month NPI for each (prefix, weight) pair, displayed per region. Rows index policy prefixes (length $0$, $1$, or $2$); columns index the humanitarian weight $w$; cell colour encodes the recommended NPI severity. The smooth left-to-right escalation at fixed prefix is the signature of the weight-conditional optimal-action map; the structure within each panel reflects how past policy and accumulated state determine the next decision. The four panels share the same colourbar.}}{20}{figure.caption.15}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:state_action_grid}{{4}{20}{Cost-optimal next-month NPI for each (prefix, weight) pair, displayed per region. Rows index policy prefixes (length $0$, $1$, or $2$); columns index the humanitarian weight $w$; cell colour encodes the recommended NPI severity. The smooth left-to-right escalation at fixed prefix is the signature of the weight-conditional optimal-action map; the structure within each panel reflects how past policy and accumulated state determine the next decision. The four panels share the same colourbar}{figure.caption.15}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {R.12}{\ignorespaces Three feasible online policies versus the unattainable hindsight optimum. $\pi ^{\text  {online}}_R$: rolling-horizon advisor with one-step lookahead under online rank-$1$ ALS gammas. $\pi ^{\text  {tree}}_R$: pooled state-action tree of \S  (A) at $w=0.5$, queried month-by-month with the THEMIS-induced state of its own prefix---this is a feasible online policy because the tree is trained once offline and the state inputs are observable. $\pi ^{\text  {actual}}_R$: the policy actually implemented; the cost is the real observed cost via \texttt  {policy\_type="actual"}. $\pi ^{\text  {HS}}_R$: hindsight-optimal sequence over all $7^3=343$ enumerations under full-window $\hat  \gamma ^{\text  {full}}$. Costs are in local currency (\$B for DE, BR, US-NY; EUR B for ES). NPI numbering follows the main paper. Online and tree-policy costs are evaluated under $\hat  \gamma ^{\text  {full}}$ on a single ground truth so the comparison isolates the policy-choice effect.}}{22}{table.caption.16}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:rolling_advisor_v2}{{R.12}{22}{Three feasible online policies versus the unattainable hindsight optimum. $\pi ^{\text {online}}_R$: rolling-horizon advisor with one-step lookahead under online rank-$1$ ALS gammas. $\pi ^{\text {tree}}_R$: pooled state-action tree of \S (A) at $w=0.5$, queried month-by-month with the THEMIS-induced state of its own prefix---this is a feasible online policy because the tree is trained once offline and the state inputs are observable. $\pi ^{\text {actual}}_R$: the policy actually implemented; the cost is the real observed cost via \texttt {policy\_type="actual"}. $\pi ^{\text {HS}}_R$: hindsight-optimal sequence over all $7^3=343$ enumerations under full-window $\hat \gamma ^{\text {full}}$. Costs are in local currency (\$B for DE, BR, US-NY; EUR B for ES). NPI numbering follows the main paper. Online and tree-policy costs are evaluated under $\hat \gamma ^{\text {full}}$ on a single ground truth so the comparison isolates the policy-choice effect}{table.caption.16}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Three-month total cost for each region under the online advisor, the tree policy ($w = 0.5$), the actual implemented policy, and the hindsight optimum. Bars decompose total cost into economic (blue) and humanitarian (red) components. Sequences below each bar follow the policy numbering of the main paper.}}{22}{figure.caption.17}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rolling_advisor_v2}{{5}{22}{Three-month total cost for each region under the online advisor, the tree policy ($w = 0.5$), the actual implemented policy, and the hindsight optimum. Bars decompose total cost into economic (blue) and humanitarian (red) components. Sequences below each bar follow the policy numbering of the main paper}{figure.caption.17}{}}
